  "cut_point": "We are given a semicircle with diameter $ PQ $ and center $ O $. A point $ R $ lies on the semicircle such that $ RM \\perp PQ $, and the measure of arc $ \\widehat{PR} $ is **twice** the measure of arc $ \\widehat{RQ} $.\n\nWe are to find the ratio $ \\frac{PM}{MQ} $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Geometry\n\n- $ PQ $ is a diameter of the semicircle.\n- $ O $ is the center, so $ OP = OQ = \\text{radius} $.\n- $ R $ is a point on the semicircle.\n- $ RM \\perp PQ $, so $ M $ is the **foot of the perpendicular** from $ R $ to $ PQ $.\n- Since $ R $ is on the semicircle, $ \\angle ORM = 90^\\circ $, and triangle $ ORM $ is a right triangle.",
  "reflection": {
    "verified_state": [
      "$PQ$ is a diameter of the semicircle.",
      "$O$ is the center, so $OP=OQ=\\text{radius}$.",
      "$R$ is a point on the semicircle.",
      "$RM\\perp PQ$, so $M$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $R$ to $PQ$."
    ],
    "error_step": "- Since $ R $ is on the semicircle, $ \\angle ORM = 90^\\circ $, and triangle $ ORM $ is a right triangle.",
    "error_reason": "A point on a semicircle does not imply $\\angle ORM=90^\\circ$. The perpendicular $RM\\perp PQ$ gives the right angle at $M$, i.e. $\\angle RMO=90^\\circ$, because $O$ and $M$ lie on $PQ$.",
    "new_direction": "Use the correct right angle at $M$, and with $P=(-r,0)$, $Q=(r,0)$, place $R$ at the central angle $60^\\circ$ from the positive x-axis since arc $\\widehat{PR}=120^\\circ$."
  }
